sics and Engineering in medicine. Adapted with permission from Cole SEA. editor. <italic>Vascular Laboratory Practice (Part III)</italic>. 1st ed. London, UK: IPEM; 2001. p. 36.</p></fn><fn id="tfn2-vhrm-5-833"><p><bold>Abbreviation:</bold> ABPI, Ankle Brachial Pressure Index.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="t2-vhrm-5-833" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Nonstandard aspects of ABPI measurement<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b42-vhrm-5-833">42</xref></p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Position of patient during measurement</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Width and level of sphygmometer cuffs</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Use of Korotkoff method vs Dinamap vs Doppler probe to define systolic pressures</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Whether to use both brachial pressures or not in calculation of ABPI</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Whether to use all three crural vessels, two, or just one in calculation of ABPI</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Whether to use highest or mean values of pressure in calculation of ABPI</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Normal ranges for ABPI ratios</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tfn3-vhrm-5-833"><p><bold>Abbreviation:</bold> ABPI, Ankle Brachial Pressure Index.</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-group></article>